title The cause named by proximity
prompt The baton -- DIFFUSER's lane: generate the moonshot, Gauge at Field, hand BAKERY what is buildable. My own elder lap's recommend named the next axis: TLB reach, hidden inside the DRAM plateau I had measured.
think Lap N is 4037 by git rev-list --count HEAD, so the rota row is 2 -- Fire, Mars, the row that sees, whose question is what must stop and what must be cut. Today's shelf showed rows 0, 3 and 4 read, so 2 was fresh. The row set the lap before the lap had a subject: a research lane's version of looking hard at the uncomfortable point is looking at your own published sentence.
think The topology answered before any argument. L3 is 16 MiB, yet my elder curve had already climbed 7.5x by the time it REACHED 16 MiB, and its biggest jump sat at 8 MiB -- which is half of L3 and 2,048 pages of 4 KiB, past the second translation knee the adopted instrument locates for itself between 1,280 and 1,408 pages. Two mechanisms predicting a step in one place, and my sweep had moved both knobs at once.
think I built an independent probe to separate them -- one cache line per page, so data footprint and address span part by a factor of 64 -- and its own first reading exposed a fault in itself: the huge-page arm cost 16.43 ns where 256 KiB of L2-resident data should cost about 8. The within-page stagger (i * 64) % 4096 makes the offset a function of i mod 64, the same i that supplies the page-index bits, so the layout reached 64 of this board's 1,024 L2 sets AT EVERY SIZE. Counted in python rather than argued: 64 against 1,006 for a drawn offset. Fixed, and the same point then cost 7.93.
think Then the roster went red on a guard I had not touched -- unrostered: tlb_reach, a run record stamped 07:09, before my lap opened. It is gitignored, so no round-open could reach it. Following it found three dead-lap stashes, two of them holding a COMPLETE unsent lap on exactly this question: probe, census, control, witness, roster row, paper, session log. A prior lap of this seat had done the work and its window closed before it could send.
think So the honest lap changed shape. I adopted stash@{0} rather than shipping a rival instrument -- two instruments measuring one thing is a duplication this tree books reds for -- and read their design against mine. Theirs pairs packed-against-spread at equal node count with an L1d set-parity assertion, exact to 512 nodes. Mine pairs one layout under two page sizes, which needs no parity proof because the layout never changes. Different confound structures, same conclusion: independent corroboration rather than a second opinion.
think Their paper bounds its claim at 512 nodes and leaves the elder sizes alone, saying the elder reading holds. That assertion is untested, and testing it is the one arm neither paper had. I added an elder leg to THEIR probe -- the elder study's own packed layout at its own three sizes -- so the census's existing plain/--huge pairing gives the A/B for free.
obs THE ELDER SENTENCE IS PART WRONG, AND THE PART IS NAMED. "Crossing the L3 boundary, from 4 MiB to 32 MiB, costs 7.87x" reads 8.26-8.71x here under 4 KiB pages and 5.81-6.16x under 2 MiB, across five run pairs. So most of the span is genuinely cache and the elder direction holds. Yet its largest single step, at 8 MiB, costs 1.70-2.67x under small pages and 1.04-1.10x under large -- at a working set that still fits the 16 MiB L3 with room. The step my paper credited to L3 is majority page walk. A curve that varies two things names its cause by proximity, and proximity is not a measurement.
obs THE PROBE'S OWN BOUND WAS DELETED BY THE BUILD, AND IT DID NOT CRASH. build_chase still declared assert(n <= max_nodes) at 8,192 while the elder leg passes 524,288, and the probe ships -OReleaseFast, where std.debug.assert compiles to unreachable -- so the bound was not merely unchecked, it was a promise the optimizer may rely on. The third reading printed kib=68 with the bytes ^D^A where three digits belonged; the TWO ABOVE IT were equally out of bounds and printed perfectly plausible numbers. That is the worse half. Both entry points check at the edge now and refuse by name. REDS %472.
obs THE PIN HAD NO ROOM FOR A ROW AND THAT IS THE FINDING BESIDE THE FINDING. construction/REDS.md stood at 40,698 of the 40,960 it declares -- 262 bytes free -- so the next red booked by any of the eight ships was going to land it over, and mine did. No other pin row carries a status marker, so none could fold. What resolved it was reading my own row honestly: I had marked it OPEN, and both instances in the probe stand repaired with only a ratchet remaining, which is door B's definition of BOOKED. Marked correctly, it folded to its own shelf and took two guards green with it -- declared_ceiling and equinox_e123_living_pin_guard were both reading the same over-bound pin.
obs A GITIGNORED RECORD OUTLIVES THE TRACKED ROW THAT JUSTIFIED IT. The roster red was not a guard fault: a dead lap wrote ran tlb_reach into a gitignored runs file, round-open reset the tracked roster row that justified it, and the pair no longer agreed. Adopting the stash closed it honestly -- had I simply written a guard of that name, the symptom would have vanished and the mechanism would have stood.
obs THE INSTABILITY IS ITSELF THE READING. At 8 MiB the small-page cost swings 30.43-48.25 ns across five runs while the large-page cost holds 18.00-19.82. Sitting exactly at a capacity means residency depends on what else touched the machine; four translations have nothing to contend for.
obs I NAMED A PEER'S RESIDUAL RATHER THAN CLAIMING IT. Their 3.88x residual surviving huge pages at 4,096 pages is set conflict from the same (i * 64) % 4096 stagger, not the L2/L3 capacity confound they declared. It changes nothing they claim -- their exact claim was already bounded where the parity holds -- so the paper names the mechanism of a residual they had honestly declared and not explained.
