obs THE ROSTER GUARD IS PINNED BY ITS OWN LAST ANSWER, AND I COULD NOT BOOK IT. standing_equipment_scan.sh counts every red in construction/standing-equipment-runs.kyri without excluding its own row, and standing_equipment_run.sh rewrites that card only at a pass's END -- so each pass reads the PREVIOUS pass's verdict for itself. Proven rather than reasoned: with that one row flipped to green in a copy, STANDING_CARD=/tmp/card_test.kyri reads runs_red=0 verdict=ok, and all 134 other guards are green. The cause that first reddened it on this tree is gone -- runs_unrostered is 0 now -- and the red remains because it reads itself. It could not be booked when I found it: an OPEN row cannot fold, the pin held 325 bytes of its 40,960, and my terse row measured 466 -- so I wrote it onto the card as surfaced-not-booked. A peer's %454 fold then freed the pin on the first rebase and I booked it as %473. On the SECOND rebase it withdrew entirely: xy had moved again, and adf682ac3 is a peer booking the same defect as %475 and REPAIRING it -- self_guard, red_self, runs_red_self, so the scan's own row prints separately and is left out of runs_red. Same defect, same proof method (remove the row, re-read the scan), two trees, inside one hour. Their number is published and mine was not, so mine goes; and my %472 renumbered to %476 by the same rule. This is the derived spine doing exactly its job, and it is also the plainest argument I have seen for the claim-at-start question the card has carried for six laps: nothing in the ledger showed that red was being worked, so two hands worked it.
