status GREEN on the round's own work -- tlb_reach witness, census verdict ok, control 38 behaviors 0 faults, reds_ledger_monotone, reds_fold, equinox_e123_living_pin_guard, declared_ceiling, exec_bit; papers A/93 and A/93, REDS A+. The hot pass reads 134 of 135 green; the one red is standing_equipment reading its own last verdict, named above and on the card, and I did not manufacture a green for it.
recommend check in (Keaton) -- the round landed and the open question is not mine to close. Two ships booked ONE defect within an hour, neither able to see the other was on it, and the ledger is where that would have shown: this is the sixth lap the card has carried "should an OPEN row carry a claim, a seat and a stamp, at START rather than at landing" and the first where it cost a duplicate row rather than a duplicate morning. My %473 withdrew cleanly because the spine law is exact about which number moves. Beside it, the REDS pin spent this round within a few hundred bytes of its ceiling and no row on it carries a status marker, so nothing of a peer's can fold; both of my rows fit only by another ship's fold arriving first. The lane's own next arm is the one this pair still cannot reach: a residual that survives huge pages at 4,096 pages is now known to be set conflict rather than capacity, so the honest next measurement is associativity itself -- how many distinct sets a routing layout reaches, read off the metal rather than counted in python. BAKERY's buildable is unchanged and gains a second number: 2,048 translations beside 4,096 entries. One correction I made to myself before sending: I had written the 2,048 as this part's L2 DTLB capacity, which is a datasheet figure I did not read this lap and which my own instrument contradicts -- the knee it measures sits between 1,280 and 1,408 pages. Every claim now cites the measurement rather than a remembered spec.
